  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how a coated slurry changes during UV curing and how this fits into a manufacturing line. In the top schematic, the slurry initially contains three mixed components: photoinitiator, oligomer, and monomer, shown as separate particles dispersed in a liquid. When UV light is applied, the reactive species begin linking together, and blue connecting lines appear between components, indicating formation of a polymer network. After curing, this network is more extensive and continuous, suggesting the slurry has solidified into a crosslinked structure rather than remaining a simple mixture.\n\nThe lower part places this chemistry in process context. It shows a long convection-oven drying zone followed by a much shorter curing zone. The drying section is labeled as being over 100 meters long, while the curing section is under 20 meters, emphasizing that UV curing can occur in a comparatively compact stage after drying. Overall, the figure conveys a transition from dispersed molecular ingredients to a cured network under UV exposure, and highlights the practical advantage of integrating a short curing step into a larger roll-to-roll or continuous coating process.",
